 "details": {
  "title": "I’ve Got a Crush on You",
  "lead": "The refrain for trumpet in F (G for the trumpet), concert F3 to C5, slow, in a 12/8 feel at about 65, set on the bars of the Fret Finder guitar arrangement, which follows Sinatra’s recording live at the Sands (1966). A short line of our own opens it, built on the tune’s “I never had the least” figure. The lead sheet has no verse, so the trumpet rests under it: sing it, or let the guitar have it. Then the pickup, the refrain, and its last half again, ending down on the low F as Sinatra does. Where Basie’s band changes the harmony, the line takes the band’s notes: E♭ over G7♭13 and A♭ over C7♭13 as Sinatra sings them, B and C♯ over the bass walking up, G♯ over B diminished 7. The song is from 1928, so it is free to use in the US.",
  "facts": [
   [
    "Song",
    "George and Ira Gershwin (1928)"
   ],
   [
    "Line",
    "The refrain from the lead sheet, on the Fret Finder page’s chords; the intro line and the last two bars are our own"
   ],
   [
    "Key",
    "F major concert (G for the trumpet), Sinatra’s key"
   ],
   [
    "Form",
